{"graph_chars": "Efficiency here means you cannot make one party better off without making another worse off. All the gains from trade have been taken. Consumer surplus is what buyers would have paid minus what they did pay: the area above price and below demand. Producer surplus is price received minus the lowest price sellers would have accepted: the area below price and above supply. Social surplus, also called economic or total surplus, is the sum. It is largest at equilibrium. A ceiling or a floor blocks some trades both sides wanted. The lost surplus is deadweight loss: money thrown away that benefits no one. A ceiling on a back-pain drug at four hundred dollars, when equilibrium was six hundred, cuts quantity from twenty thousand users to fifteen thousand, transfers some producer surplus to consumers, and creates deadweight loss. Alfred Marshall said asking whether demand or supply sets the price is like asking which blade of the scissors cuts the paper. Both blades always cut.", "graph_times": [[0.0, 0.0629], [0.0629, 0.1259], [0.1259, 0.1888], [0.1888, 0.2517], [0.2517, 0.3147], [0.3147, 0.3776], [0.3776, 0.4405], [0.4405, 0.5035], [0.5035, 0.5664], [0.5664, 0.6293], [0.6293, 0.6923], [0.6923, 0.7552], [0.7552, 0.8181], [0.8181, 0.8811], [0.8811, 0.944], [0.944, 1.0069], [1.0069, 1.0699], [1.0699, 1.1328], [1.1328, 1.1957], [1.1957, 1.2587], [1.2587, 1.3216], [1.3216, 1.3845], [1.3845, 1.4475], [1.4475, 1.5104], [1.5104, 1.5733], [1.5733, 1.6363], [1.6363, 1.6992], [1.6992, 1.7621], [1.7621, 1.8251], [1.8251, 1.888], [1.888, 1.9509], [1.9509, 2.0138], [2.0138, 2.0768], [2.0768, 2.1397], [2.1397, 2.2026], [2.2026, 2.2656], [2.2656, 2.3285], [2.3285, 2.3914], [2.3914, 2.4544], [2.4544, 2.5173], [2.5173, 2.5802], [2.5802, 2.6432], [2.6432, 2.7061], [2.7061, 2.769], [2.769, 2.832], [2.832, 2.8949], [2.8949, 2.9578], [2.9578, 3.0208], [3.0208, 3.0837], [3.0837, 3.1466], [3.1466, 3.2096], [3.2096, 3.2725], [3.2725, 3.3354], [3.3354, 3.3984], [3.3984, 3.4613], [3.